extends /views/templates/layouts/_base

append variables
	- pagetitle = "Styling | Background"
	- pagedescription = "The background colour in Tablechart, which lighter lines and donut slices are mixed with."

block content
	.lane
		.container
			.page-header
				h1 Background
				p.lead The colour behind a chart, which lighter lines and slices are mixed with.

	+section("What uses the background colour")
		p Tablechart measures the background colour behind a chart: the first background that is not transparent, such as that of the page, a card or a section. In Reveal.js, the #[code data-background-color] of a slide counts as its background. The chart itself stays transparent.
		p Each line after the first is lighter than the one before it, and so is each donut slice. The lighter colour is the line or slice colour mixed with the background colour. Borders of scatter markers have that colour too, and so does a number inside a bar, if it has enough contrast. See #[a(href=url('/styling/text.html#numbers-inside-bars-or-columns')) Numbers inside bars or columns].
		p If you set a colour for each line or slice with a #[a(href=url('/styling/colours.html')) palette], the background colour is not used for them.
		p These parts are cut out of the chart, so they show the real background:
		ul
			li the inside of open points on a line;
			li the badge of a bracket or trend arrow;
			li the gaps between donut slices;
			li the break in a scale.

		p In this example, the box has gotten a dark background:

		+style.
			.dark {
				background: #16323f;
				color: #f3efe9;
				padding: 1em;
				--tablechart-slice-color: #f3efe9;
			}

		+chart(null, '1')
			figure.dark(data-chart="donut")
				table
					tbody
						each row in [['Juiced', '38.2'], ['Eaten fresh', '18.1'], ['Sold whole', '12.4'], ['Composted', '5.8']]
							tr
								th= row[0]
								td= row[1]

	+section("A background that cannot be measured")
		p An image or a gradient has no single colour to measure. If a chart is on one, set #[code --tablechart-surface] to the colour that comes closest. It is then used instead of the measured colour.

		+codeblock('CSS').
			.hero {
				background: url(apples.jpg);
				--tablechart-surface: #2b3a2f;
			}
